= Background and Related Work <sec:background>

This chapter places the thesis between two research traditions that rarely meet. The first is multi-robot coordination, where paths, velocities and priorities are computed from geometric models of the robots and the floor. The second is the use of large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs) as planners for robots, where the decision comes from a model that reads text and images. The coordinator studied in this thesis takes something from each. A VLM looks at the scene and names a congestion situation and a strategy, and a deterministic layer turns that name into commands that a conventional navigation stack can execute safely (@sec:design).

The chapter is organised around the question each body of work leaves open, not as a catalogue. @sec:bg-mapf covers centralised path planning and congestion-aware planning for warehouse fleets. @sec:bg-reactive turns to reactive collision avoidance and the navigation stack used in this project. @sec:bg-deadlock reviews how deadlock and congestion have been defined and resolved. @sec:bg-vlm surveys language and vision-language models as robot planners and coordinators, and @sec:bg-grounding the methods that ground or check their plans. Each of these sections ends with the gap that the thesis addresses. @sec:bg-comparison compares the closest systems with this thesis along five axes, and @sec:bg-gap draws the gaps together into the motivation for the research questions.

A note on scope. The literature on LLM-based robotics moves quickly. Statements below of the form "no published system does X" are bounded by the literature searches made for this project during autumn 2026. They mean "to our knowledge", not "provably not".

== Multi-Agent Path Finding and Congestion-Aware Planning <sec:bg-mapf>

*The MAPF problem.* Multi-agent path finding (MAPF) asks for collision-free paths that move a team of agents from their start locations to their goals on a shared graph. #cite(<stern2019multi>, form: "prose") standardised the definitions and benchmarks of the field. Their formulation names several kinds of conflict between agents, among them vertex conflicts (two agents in the same place at the same time) and swapping conflicts (two agents exchanging places along one edge). Difficulty is measured by whether a solver finds a solution within a time limit; the formulation defines no measure of congestion. Classical MAPF is a discrete abstraction. Agents occupy one vertex per time step, time advances synchronously, and physical spacing is absorbed into the size of a grid cell.

*Optimal and suboptimal search.* Conflict-Based Search (CBS) @sharon2015conflict is the reference optimal solver: a two-level algorithm whose construction guarantees an optimal solution. Because optimal MAPF is NP-hard, optimal solvers do not scale to large teams, and #cite(<barer2014suboptimal>, form: "prose") relaxed the optimality conditions of CBS to obtain bounded-suboptimal variants that trade a small loss in solution quality for a large reduction in run time. A second line of work gives up optimality from the start in exchange for speed. Priority Inheritance with Backtracking (PIBT) @okumura2022priority plans one step at a time under adaptive priorities and is designed for iterative, online use in settings such as automated warehouses with hundreds of agents. LaCAM and its anytime extension LaCAM\* @okumura2023improving use lazy successor generation to find solutions quickly for large teams, while converging to the optimum when given more time.

*Lifelong MAPF in warehouses.* A warehouse does not pose one MAPF instance but a stream of them, because robots receive new goals as soon as they finish old ones. #cite(<ma2017lifelong>, form: "prose") formalised this setting as multi-agent pickup and delivery (MAPD) and proved that their decoupled token-passing algorithms solve every "well-formed" instance, a structural condition on the layout and on where agents may rest. #cite(<li2020lifelong>, form: "prose") proposed Rolling-Horizon Collision Resolution (RHCR), which decomposes lifelong MAPF into a sequence of windowed instances. Each resolves collisions only within a bounded time horizon, and the solver replans periodically, interleaving planning with execution. The bounded horizon is what lets RHCR scale, and it is also a source of failure. The authors show that two agents in a corridor can keep waiting for each other under a short horizon, and they accept that a throughput-maximising policy can starve a single agent at a busy intersection.

*Congestion as an explicit objective.* Several recent works treat congestion itself as the quantity to control. #cite(<chen2023traffic>, form: "prose") observe that MAPF algorithms typically plan free-flow optimal paths and thereby create congestion, and they guide agents along congestion-avoiding paths instead. Guidance graphs @zhang2024guidance encode such guidance as edge weights on the map and optimise those weights automatically, which improves the throughput of several lifelong MAPF algorithms. #cite(<asadi2025congestion>, form: "prose") add a learned congestion prediction to an online MAPD algorithm, with a deep network that approximates congestion independently of the number of agents. At fleet scale, DeepFleet @agaskar2025deepfleet trains foundation models on movement data from Amazon warehouses (robot positions, goals and interactions) to support coordination and planning. Most recently, #cite(<tang2026late>, form: "prose") used LLM coding agents offline to specialise a general warehouse planner for one site. The agents raised throughput and rediscovered known MAPF mechanisms such as corridor-exit priorities, so the language model acts there as a planner engineer, not as an online decision maker. The same study also reports the limits of such specialisation: the gains mostly vanished under an outbound-heavy demand mix, and some patches multiplied the planner's compute. A rule such as "the robot leaving a corridor goes first" is therefore a natural classical reference point for any learned or model-based coordinator. Warehouse robot fleets of this kind trace back to the Kiva system #todo[cite: Wurman 2008 Kiva warehouse coordination].

*Gap.* MAPF and its congestion-aware variants are strong where their assumptions hold: a known graph, synchronous discrete time, and robots that execute plans as computed. Their representation of congestion is numerical (edge weights, densities, predicted occupancy). As far as we found, none of the systems above looks at the scene and says what kind of jam has formed and why. More importantly for this thesis, they plan the fleet in advance. Once a jam has formed among robots that run their own local planners, as in a ROS 2 fleet, a MAPF solver has no direct way to unpick it, because its model of the fleet (a graph, discrete time, exact plan execution) no longer matches what the robots are doing. An overhead view supplies what such a model lacks: where the robots actually stand, how they are blocked, and what else occupies the aisle. The thesis therefore does not replace global planning. It asks whether a semantic layer can recognise and clear a jam after it has formed, and then hand control back to the robots' own planners.

== Reactive Collision Avoidance and Navigation Stacks <sec:bg-reactive>

*Reactive methods.* At the other end of the spectrum, each robot chooses its next motion from what it senses now, without a joint plan. Artificial potential fields @khatib1986real treat the goal as an attractor and obstacles as repellers. They run in real time, are cheap to compute, and are known to suffer from local minima; the potential-field approach has also been extended to dynamic environments @ge2002dynamic. The dynamic window approach @fox1997dynamic derives its velocity choice directly from the motion dynamics of the robot, considering only velocities the robot can reach within a short window. For many agents moving among one another, Reciprocal Velocity Obstacles @vandenberg2008reciprocal assume that the other agents reason about collision avoidance in the same way, so that the effort of avoidance is shared. The authors show safe and oscillation-free motion for hundreds of agents in dense environments. The reciprocal formulation was later extended to $n$ bodies as Optimal Reciprocal Collision Avoidance (ORCA) @vandenberg2011reciprocal. Swarm robotics as a field, reviewed from an engineering perspective by #cite(<brambilla2013swarm>, form: "prose"), builds on such local interaction rules.

*Where reactive methods stall.* Local rules decide how to avoid a collision but not who should give way, and in narrow passages and symmetric encounters this matters. #cite(<trautman2010unfreezing>, form: "prose") describe the "freezing robot" problem: once the environment becomes complex enough, a planner may decide that every path forward is unsafe and stop, although a feasible path exists. They argue that agents in crowds avoid this by cooperating, making room for one another. For controllers based on control barrier functions (CBFs), #cite(<grover2019deadlock>, form: "prose") and #cite(<grover2022before>, form: "prose") show that deadlock is an equilibrium of the closed-loop dynamics, characterised by a balance of forces on the robots, and that the deadlock set lies on the boundary of the safe set. The robots stop because continuing would violate safety. The related-work review of #cite(<grover2022before>, form: "prose") also reports that velocity-obstacle methods have been found empirically prone to deadlock in symmetric scenes. Reactive safety and progress are therefore in tension precisely in the situations a warehouse produces often: two robots meeting in an aisle, or several robots arriving at a junction.

*ROS 2 and Nav2.* The robots in this thesis run the standard ROS 2 navigation stack. ROS 2 @macenski2022robot is the redesigned Robot Operating System, built for modular, scalable and reliable robot software. Nav2 @macenski2020marathon @nav2docs is its navigation system. It orchestrates navigation tasks with behaviour trees, plans paths over costmaps, follows them with local controllers, and runs recovery behaviours when progress fails. Nav2 is a single-robot system: each robot plans for itself and sees other robots as obstacles in its costmaps. A design observation of this thesis, not a claim of the Nav2 authors, is that the standard recovery behaviours (waiting, backing up, clearing a costmap) are local to one robot and do not represent the fact that the obstacle is another robot making its own decisions. Two robots that face each other and run the same recovery policy can therefore reproduce the symmetric situation that caused the stall.

*Layering deliberation over reaction.* The remedies proposed for reactive deadlock already point towards a layered design. The provably correct resolution of #cite(<grover2019deadlock>, form: "prose") is a decision procedure that runs on top of the reactive CBF controller and acts only once the robots are stuck. #cite(<cap2016provably>, form: "prose") note that reactive collision avoidance can lead to deadlocks between robots and instead execute coordinated plans with a control strategy that only re-times them. RHCR @li2020lifelong interleaves slow, periodic planning with continuous execution. Common to all three is a slower decision layer that sets an order or a timing, and a faster layer that keeps the motion safe. The two-layer architecture of this thesis adopts the same split, with a model-based semantic layer in the slow role.

*Gap.* Reactive avoidance and Nav2 give fast, safe local motion, and this thesis keeps them as its Layer 1 for that reason. What they lack is a view of the whole situation and an authority that can break symmetry by choosing an order: which robot backs off, which passes first, and when everyone resumes. The thesis adds that authority above an unmodified navigation stack instead of replacing the local controller.

== Deadlock and Congestion: Taxonomies and Resolution Strategies <sec:bg-deadlock>

*Deadlock.* The classical treatment of deadlock comes from operating systems, where #cite(<coffman1971system>, form: "prose") surveyed the "deadly embrace" problem of multiprogramming systems. The four conditions associated with that work (mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait) carry over to robots once the contested resource is floor space. Two robots cannot occupy the same aisle segment; each holds its footprint while waiting for the next segment; safety layers forbid pushing another robot aside; and blocked robots can wait on one another in a cycle. This mapping is this thesis's reading, not a result of the cited survey. It explains why a jam does not dissolve on its own: breaking it means releasing one of the conditions, most practically by making one robot give up space it holds.

*Congestion as a flow phenomenon.* Congestion is broader than deadlock. Experiments with robot swarms moving in single file @alonsollanes2024single reproduce the fundamental diagram of traffic flow, with a transition from free flow to congested traffic as density rises and with stop-and-go waves. Above a further density, the average jam duration and the number of robots involved diverge, marking a second transition to total congestion. For swarms converging on a common target area, #cite(<passos2022congestion>, form: "prose") design decentralised algorithms that maximise the throughput of the target region, for example by forming a queue. These works define congestion through flow, throughput and density, quantities measured over many robots and long intervals.

*Taxonomies of conflict scenes.* For small groups of robots in constrained spaces, the most developed vocabulary is that of social mini-games. #cite(<chandra2023deadlock>, form: "prose") formalise doorways, hallways and corridor intersections as non-cooperative, non-communicative, partially observable navigation problems with conflicting agents, and they evaluate with measures such as success rate, collision rate, stop time, time-to-goal and flow rate. Their survey @chandra2025multi argues that this area needs its own taxonomy, definitions and evaluation protocols, and it catalogues existing solvers accordingly. The scenes of this thesis, a head-on encounter in a narrow aisle and a multi-robot jam at a junction, belong to this family. The MAPF conflict types of @sec:bg-mapf supply the corresponding vocabulary on graphs.

*Resolution strategies.* The literature resolves deadlock in four broad ways, and each corresponds to a strategy family in the vocabulary of this thesis (@sec:design).

- _Perturbation onto deadlock-free states._ The CBF analyses above lead to decentralised resolution. #cite(<grover2019deadlock>, form: "prose") give a provably correct algorithm that moves the robots out of deadlock towards their goals, and #cite(<chandra2023deadlock>, form: "prose") let each robot perturb its state pre-emptively onto "liveness sets" of deadlock-free states. Both are validated on physical robots, and both work by breaking the symmetry of the encounter.
- _Ordering by reservation or priority._ In autonomous intersection management @dresner2008multiagent, a central manager grants vehicles space-time reservations through an intersection, by default on a first-come, first-served basis. Priority-based MAPF solvers such as PIBT @okumura2022priority resolve conflicts through an explicit, adaptive order.
- _Recede and yield._ A-DRIVE @aoki2022drive detects deadlock among automated vehicles at intersections and single-track road segments and recovers by having lower-priority vehicles yield and back away, with a communication-based variant and a perception-based variant for mixed traffic. It is the closest classical analogue of the yield-and-back-off manoeuvres used in this thesis.
- _Re-timing instead of re-routing._ #cite(<cap2016provably>, form: "prose") show that robots executing precomputed coordinated trajectories remain safe and deadlock-free under delaying disturbances when they adjust only their timing along fixed paths.

*Detection.* Before a deadlock can be resolved it must be noticed, and the methods above detect it in simple ways. A-DRIVE @aoki2022drive tracks each vehicle through a small set of states and declares deadlock when vehicles wait beyond a threshold time while blocking one another. The extended work of Garg et al. @garg2026online triggers on a fleet that has slowed down while still far from its goals. In both cases detection is a cheap progress test, and the expensive reasoning is reserved for the moment after it fires. The coordinator of this thesis follows the same division: a stall trigger in the orchestrator decides when to call the model, and the model decides what to do (@sec:design).

Waiting, rerouting and convoying complete the usual repertoire. They are easy to state, but whether they help depends on the geometry of the scene.

*Gap.* Taxonomies describe scenes, and resolution methods act on them. In the classical resolvers above, the link between the two is largely a designer's choice, made in advance, of which mechanism applies to which scene. These resolvers are mostly decentralised and geometric, and each tends to be built and evaluated for a particular family of scenes. The thesis asks whether a model can make that link online: look at a scene, say which kind of conflict it is, and pick a strategy from a closed vocabulary that a deterministic layer knows how to execute. Whether the model's choice is a good one is an empirical question, and the grounding layer of @sec:bg-grounding exists because the answer may be "not always".

== Language and Vision-Language Models as Robot Planners and Coordinators <sec:bg-vlm>

*From language models to embodied models.* Large language models carry broad semantic knowledge but no experience of a particular robot or environment. SayCan @ahn2022grounding made this gap explicit and addressed it by combining the language model's judgement of whether a skill is useful with a learned estimate of whether the skill can succeed in the current state. Later models took in perception directly. PaLM-E @driess2023palm interleaves visual and continuous state inputs with text in one embodied multimodal language model, and RT-2 @brohan2023vision co-fine-tunes vision-language models on robot trajectories so that they output actions as text tokens. These are end-to-end models, mostly for single-robot manipulation, and are background here rather than competitors. Closer to the needs of this thesis are VLMs with stronger spatial reasoning. SpatialVLM @chen2024spatialvlm trains on large-scale generated spatial question answering to give VLMs quantitative spatial judgements. The Gemini Robotics family adds a dedicated embodied-reasoning model, Gemini Robotics-ER @gemini2025robotics, continued in the 1.5 report @gemini2025robotics15. The live experiments of this thesis use a later model of that line, Gemini Robotics-ER 2 @google2026geminier2 @google2026geminier. Earlier work and offline analyses in the project used GPT-4o @openai2024system and Claude models @anthropic2026claude. Visual prompting is a further lever. Set-of-Mark prompting @yang2023mark overlays alphanumeric marks on image regions, and with these marks GPT-4V outperformed a fully fine-tuned model on a referring-expression benchmark in a zero-shot setting. The annotated overhead frame used in this thesis applies the same idea, with robot identities drawn onto the image.

*LLMs as multi-robot planners.* A first wave of work uses LLMs to plan for teams of robots, mostly for household and manipulation tasks given as natural-language instructions. SMART-LLM @kannan2023smart is centralised: one model decomposes an instruction, forms coalitions and allocates tasks through staged programmatic prompts, and it is evaluated in simulation and real-world scenarios. RoCo @mandi2023roco gives each robot arm its own LLM agent. The agents discuss strategy in dialogue and propose sub-task plans and waypoints, and environment feedback such as collision checks prompts them to revise. COHERENT @liu2024coherent runs a proposal-execution-feedback-adjustment loop between a central task assigner and per-robot executors in a heterogeneous team. LaMMA-P @zhang2024lamma lets the language model allocate tasks and generate PDDL problems, and a classical heuristic planner then produces each robot's actions. #cite(<chen2023scalable>, form: "prose") compare centralised, decentralised and two hybrid communication frameworks and find that a hybrid achieves better success and scales better in their tasks. The survey of #cite(<li2025large>, form: "prose") organises the field by level (high-level task allocation, mid-level motion planning, low-level action generation, human intervention) and names its main obstacles: limited mathematical reasoning, hallucination, latency and the lack of robust benchmarks.

In the vocabulary of #cite(<chen2023scalable>, form: "prose"), the coordinator of this thesis is centralised at the semantic layer: one model sees the whole scene and decides for all robots involved in a conflict. Below that layer, however, nothing is centralised. Each robot keeps its own navigation stack, and the central layer only issues occasional commands when a conflict has been detected. The four obstacles named by the survey map directly onto this design. Limited spatial and mathematical reasoning is why the model chooses a strategy label instead of computing distances and waypoints. Hallucination is why its output passes a closed vocabulary and a schema before it reaches any robot. Latency is why it is called only when local control has stalled, at a rate far below the control loop. The lack of benchmarks is why this thesis builds and preregisters its own evaluation scenes (Chapter 5).

*Foundation models for deadlock and navigation.* Only a few works point foundation models at the problem studied here. #cite(<garg2024foundation>, form: "prose") propose a hierarchical framework for connected multi-robot systems in which an LLM or a VLM resolves deadlocks by assigning a leader and a set of waypoints, and a distributed low-level policy based on graph neural networks follows them safely. The extended version of this line @garg2026online uses the VLM reactively. It is called only when a velocity-based detector finds that the robots have slowed while still far from their goals, it is prompted with a top-view image of the environment observed so far, and safety is left to a provably safe low-level controller. The authors also test the spatial reasoning of the candidate models before planning, by asking them to locate obstacles and to place waypoints in free space, and they contrast their approach with earlier work that converts images to text for an LLM. Its experiments are in simulation. Of all published work found, this line is structurally closest to the present thesis: a stall trigger, a top-view image, a semantic decision by a VLM, and a safe executor below it. LLMDR @seo2025llmdr places an LLM on top of learned MAPF policies on grid benchmarks. The LLM detects deadlock from agents' movement patterns and assigns resolution strategies, and prioritised planning turns them into collision-free moves. #cite(<dwedar2026safe>, form: "prose") present a centralised framework for a quadruped and a camera-less vehicle. A VLM interprets the quadruped's camera stream, an LLM proposes high-level directives, and a reachability gate alone holds authority over physical commands; when a check fails, the proposed motion is rejected. SwarmVLM @zafar2025swarmvlm uses a VLM with retrieval-augmented generation to choose impedance-control parameters for a drone leading a ground robot in a warehouse, observed by a ceiling-mounted camera, with real-world trials. Its VLM tunes control parameters; it does not resolve conflicts between robots.

*Gap.* Three observations follow. First, the multi-robot LLM planners above plan tasks from instructions. They do not detect and classify a traffic conflict that has arisen unprompted on a warehouse floor. Second, the works that do address deadlock either operate on grids with text input (LLMDR) or in simulation with learned controllers (Garg et al.). The one hardware system that combines a VLM with a formal gate (Dwedar et al.) contains no congestion or deadlock scenarios, by the project's reading of the paper. Third, as far as we found, none of them feeds a fixed overhead camera frame of a warehouse fleet to a VLM in order to choose among named congestion strategies that are executed through a standard navigation stack. This thesis occupies that position. It does not claim novelty for central deadlock recovery, for a fast–slow two-rate architecture, or for a propose-and-verify loop. A-DRIVE recovers from deadlock by commanded yielding, LLMDR and Garg et al. resolve deadlock from a central model, RHCR already interleaves periodic planning with execution, and RoCo already verifies proposed multi-robot motion.

== Grounding, Retrieval and Plan Feasibility <sec:bg-grounding>

*Language models do not plan reliably on their own.* #cite(<valmeekam2023planning>, form: "prose") evaluated LLMs on planning domains similar to those of the International Planning Competition and found their ability to generate executable plans autonomously to be limited. In the same study, LLM plans did help when they served as heuristic guidance for sound planners and when external verifiers fed back on them. When LLM plans were given as seed plans to a local-search planner that repairs flaws, the planner fixed them with relative ease, but by the authors' own edit-distance comparison much of each final plan was due to the planner's edits rather than to the model. The point carries over to this thesis: when a deterministic layer repairs a model's plan, the credit for the executed plan has to be divided between the two. #cite(<kambhampati2024llms>, form: "prose") turn this into the position of "LLM-Modulo" frameworks: the LLM is a source of approximate knowledge and candidate plans, and external model-based verifiers check the candidates in a tight loop with the model. A study of multiple-choice evaluation adds a caution relevant to any model that chooses from a fixed menu. Modern LLMs prefer particular option identifiers, such as "Option A", largely independently of the option content @zheng2023large.

*Grounding by translation into a sound planner.* One family keeps the language model out of the correctness argument. LLM+P @liu2023empowering translates a natural-language problem into PDDL, solves it with a classical planner, and translates the plan back. LaMMA-P @zhang2024lamma applies the same idea to multi-robot task allocation. Correctness then comes from the planner, and the model's role is to formalise the problem.

*Grounding by checking and constraint enforcement.* A second family lets the model plan and checks the plan before or during execution. SayCan @ahn2022grounding filters skills by their affordance in the current state. The Safety Chip @yang2023plug encodes natural-language constraints in linear temporal logic, monitors an LLM agent's decisions and prunes unsafe actions. VerifyLLM @grigorev2025verifyllm translates instructions into temporal logic and uses an LLM to analyse a plan for inconsistencies before execution. RoboGuard @ravichandran2025safety grounds predefined safety rules in the robot's environment as temporal-logic specifications and resolves conflicts between these and a possibly unsafe plan through control synthesis, so that the plan is corrected while violating the user's preferences as little as possible. EvoPlan @nukapotula2026evoplan lets an LLM propose and repair plans while programmatic validators decide which survive, and it checks waypoints in a Nav2-based execution loop. In multi-robot settings, the reachability gate of #cite(<dwedar2026safe>, form: "prose") and RoCo's environment feedback @mandi2023roco belong to the same family.

*Grounding by retrieval.* Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) @lewis2020retrieval combines a parametric language model with retrieval from an explicit, non-parametric memory, motivated in part by the difficulty of updating a model's knowledge and of providing provenance for its outputs. Toolformer @schick2023toolformer shows that language models can learn when to call external tools. In robotics, SwarmVLM @zafar2025swarmvlm retrieves stored control parameters keyed on what its VLM reports about the scene. Retrieval grounds an answer in stored cases. It does not, by itself, check whether the retrieved action is feasible in the current geometry.

*Gap.* The feasibility literature is mostly about single-robot household and manipulation tasks, where the checked object is a sequence of symbolic actions. A congestion manoeuvre for several mobile robots in a narrow aisle is a different object. Whether "robot 2 yields" is feasible depends on where the robots stand, how much free space lies behind each of them, and which way the corridor runs. When a check fails, most systems either reject the plan (Dwedar et al.) or return it to the model for another attempt (RoCo, LLM-Modulo). For a coordinator that is called at a low rate and must never move robots on an unchecked plan, neither is sufficient on its own. The project proposal planned retrieval for this step. The evaluated system instead uses a deterministic, geometry-driven grounder that checks each proposed manoeuvre against the scene, repairs or replaces it where it is infeasible, refuses when no safe manoeuvre exists, and logs which of these happened (@sec:design). Two questions are left open by the literature: how often a VLM's choice survives such a check unchanged, and whether grounding, rather than detection, limits the pipeline.

