Three patterns stand out. First, the input column divides the field. Most multi-robot language planners work on text, and the image-based systems see either a top view built from observations (Garg et al.), one robot's own camera (Dwedar et al.), or a ceiling camera used to describe obstacles (SwarmVLM). Second, every system that moves real robots places something between the model and the actuators: a learned controller, a planner, a gate or a potential field. This thesis follows that consensus rather than departing from it. Third, the grounding column shows the choices described in @sec:bg-grounding. Checks reject, re-prompt, or hand the problem to a planner. None of the compared systems reports how much of each executed plan came from the model verbatim, how much was repaired, and how much was replaced.

== Research Gap <sec:bg-gap>

The five sections above leave one combined gap. Global planners (@sec:bg-mapf) prevent jams by planning ahead, but they cannot unpick a jam that has formed among robots running their own navigation stacks. Reactive avoidance and Nav2 (@sec:bg-reactive) keep robots safe, but they cannot decide who gives way in a symmetric encounter. Classical deadlock resolvers (@sec:bg-deadlock) are largely built for scene families chosen at design time. Language and vision-language planners (@sec:bg-vlm) bring flexible scene understanding, but as far as we found they have not been used to classify and resolve congestion from a fixed overhead camera above a standard navigation stack. Plan-checking methods (@sec:bg-grounding) show that model output must be grounded, but they were developed mainly for symbolic single-robot tasks and usually reject or re-prompt rather than repair.

The research questions of this thesis (@sec:ch5-rq) follow from this gap. Whether a VLM can classify congestion correctly from an annotated overhead frame tests the premise of @sec:bg-vlm. Whether the strategies it picks, and the robot it chooses to yield, actually clear the jam tests whether a model can link scene to strategy online, as @sec:bg-deadlock asks. Whether it uses information no classical rule has, and whether feedback on a failed command changes its next decision, test what a semantic layer adds over the geometric methods of @sec:bg-mapf and @sec:bg-reactive. Finally, because the evaluated system grounds every decision deterministically, the experiments must also show what the grounding layer contributes. Any resolution is therefore credited to the pipeline (VLM and grounding together), not to the model alone. Chapter 3 presents the design, and Chapter 5 describes how these questions are measured.
